<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">French Directory</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Directoire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French Directory was the five-member executive that governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a> from November 1795 to November 1799 under the Constitution of the Year III. It faced financial crisis, corruption and repeated coups against royalists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobin-club">Jacobins</a>, while its armies, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, created sister republics across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Bonaparte overthrew it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-18-brumaire">Coup of 18 Brumaire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Directory grew out of the Constitution of the Year III, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> debated from 4 July to 17 August 1795 and adopted on 22 August. It was a long document of 377 articles, against 124 in the constitution of 1793. Of five million eligible voters, 1,057,390 approved it in a referendum and 49,978 opposed it. The Convention required that five hundred of its own members remain in the new legislature.</p><p>In October 1795 royalists and constitutional monarchists, hostile to that rule, tried to seize power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and were put down. The elections that followed returned 379 members of the old Convention, and the Convention required that every Director be a former member who had voted for the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Directory began with an empty treasury and almost worthless assignats. It stopped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> them in 1796, replaced them with the mandats territoriaux, which also collapsed, and then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold coin, which brought falling prices and wages. Bread shortages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1796 fed the Conspiracy of the Equals of François-Noël Babeuf, which the government crushed.</p><p>The main task was the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The Directory annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and set up twenty-nine short-lived sister republics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, which sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> money and art treasures. By the Treaty of Campoformio of 17 October 1797 France received the Ionian Islands, Belgium and the left bank of the Rhine as far south as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andernach">Andernach</a>.</p><p>The royalists triumphed in the elections of 1797 and were then put down by a coup, and in 1798 the Directors disqualified Jacobin candidates. In 1799 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> formed a Second Coalition. Bonaparte returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Abbé Sieyès</a> and others, overthrew the Directory on 9 November 1799.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Five Directors held executive power. The presidency was an entirely ceremonial post, held first by Jean-François Rewbell, chosen by lot on 2 November 1795, and passed on every three months. Louis Gohier was the last president.</p><p>Under the Directory the middle and upper classes replaced the nobility at the top of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> society, and enormous fortunes were made by army suppliers and property speculators. Young Parisians known as Incroyables and Merveilleuses dressed in extravagant costumes and attended the Bals des victimes. The Goncourt brothers reported 640 balls in Paris in 1797 alone. Crime and highway robbery increased, and the death penalty was applied to robberies on the main roads.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French Directory” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frederick William I created the General <b>Directory</b> in 1723 to run finance, war and the royal domains and recruited officials trained in cameralist administration, while the provincial estates kept their forms but lost most of their power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In early 1798 the <b>French Directory</b> sent armies into the Confederacy; Vaud proclaimed itself free, and after the defeat of Bernese forces at the Battle of Grauholz on 5 March 1798, Bern surrendered and the old order collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1796 Józef Wybicki, a leading moderate émigré politician, proposed Polish Legions within the French Revolutionary Army and persuaded General Jan Henryk Dąbrowski, a hero of the 1794 Kościuszko Uprising, to put the plan to the <b>French Directory</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Directory</b> governed from 1795 amid corruption and coups.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a></li></ul></section>
